+Standard differentiable rasterizer for 3D Gaussians using EWA splatting and Jacobian linearization [S1]. It sets itself apart by using first-order approximations for projection, making it faster for simple pinhole cameras but prone to distortion errors [S1]. Choose it when rendering standard pinhole camera sequences where maximum performance is critical [S1]. [2813-3d-gaussian-splatting-differentiable-rasterizer.md]
+
+### Gaussian Splat Rasterization (Vertex Shader)
+Rasterization method implemented directly in vertex shaders for GPU acceleration [G]. It sets itself apart by leveraging fixed-function graphics pipeline stages for throughput [G]. Choose it when hardware acceleration and low CPU overhead are prioritized [G]. [3253-gaussian-splat-rasterization-vertex-shader.md]
+
+### Masked-Rasterization
+Rasterization technique that uses spatial masks to skip empty or transparent regions [G]. It sets itself apart by reducing fill-rate bottlenecks through early discard [G]. Choose it when scenes contain large transparent or empty areas [G]. [4366-masked-rasterization.md]
+
+### Differentiable Radiative Rasterization
+Rasterization method that differentiates through radiative transfer equations [G]. It sets itself apart by supporting physically based light transport during rasterization [G]. Choose it when accurate lighting simulation is required alongside rasterization [G]. [4526-differentiable-radiative-rasterization.md]
+
+### 3D/2D Hybrid Gaussian Rasterization with Fallback Sigma
+Hybrid approach combining 3D Gaussian splatting with 2D Gaussian fallbacks [G]. It sets itself apart by using 2D Gaussians to handle extreme perspective distortion or depth ambiguity [G]. Choose it when standard 3D splatting fails under extreme camera angles [G]. [4812-3d-2d-hybrid-gaussian-rasterization-with-fallback-sigma.md]
+
+### Opacity Rendering with SH Colors
+Rendering method that decouples opacity estimation from spherical harmonic color evaluation [G]. It sets itself apart by preventing color bleeding through alpha channels [G]. Choose it when accurate alpha compositing is critical for compositing [G]. [7480-opacity-rendering-with-sh-colors.md]
+
+### RoofGS Quadratic Form Expansion and Coefficient Folding
+Method that expands Gaussian quadratic forms into folded coefficients for faster evaluation [G]. It sets itself apart by reducing per-pixel computation cost through algebraic simplification [G]. Choose it when rendering performance is bottlenecked by Gaussian evaluation [G]. [10258-roofgs-quadratic-form-expansion-and-coefficient-folding.md]
+
+### Transmittance of Gaussian Splatting
+Technique that computes exact transmittance along viewing rays through Gaussian volumes [G]. It sets itself apart by replacing approximate alpha blending with physically accurate opacity accumulation [G]. Choose it when accurate transparency and depth ordering are required [G]. [10263-transmittance-of-gaussian-splatting.md]
+
+### Rendering Pipeline and Configuration
+Standard configuration framework for managing rendering parameters and pipeline stages [G]. It sets itself apart by providing a unified interface for tuning rasterization and compositing settings [G]. Choose it when reproducible and configurable rendering setups are needed [G]. [11406-rendering-pipeline-and-configuration.md]
+
+### Differentiable Backpropagation Mechanisms
+Methods for computing gradients through the rendering pipeline for optimization [G]. It sets itself apart by enabling end-to-end training of Gaussian parameters via automatic differentiation [G]. Choose it when training or optimizing scene representations [G]. [11407-differentiable-backpropagation-mechanisms.md]
 
